          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em sampling map
            is an assignment that takes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+distribution"} probability distribution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            on a space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            distributed according to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p It is a mathematical abstraction of the idea of flipping a coin, or of rolling a die (which are fair, or for which we know the bias): the outcome is random, but we know exactly the distribution it follows.
          %h2#in_measuretheoretic_probability In measure-theoretic probability
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measures"} probability measures
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %strong sampling map
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernel"} Markov kernel
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi samp
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} samp:P X\to X
            defined for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\in P X
            and every measurable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\subseteq X
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi samp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  samp(A|p) \;\coloneqq\; p(A) .
          %p
            We can interpret this kernel as of “taking a sample from the distribution
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %h3#example Example
          %p
            Given a number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in[0,1]
            %strong Bernoulli random variable
            with bias
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Bern
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bern(b)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/random+variable"} random variable
            which is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            with probability
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            with probability
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1-b
            = succeed "\u201D.)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
          %p
            We can view the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            as parametrizing all the possible probability distributions on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]\cong P\{0,1\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bern
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bern
            induces a Markov kernel
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\{0,1\}\to\{0,1\}
          %h3#in_terms_of_the_giry_monad In terms of the Giry monad
          %p
            The sampling map is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit"} counit
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} category of Markov kernels
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Meas"} category of measurable spaces
          %p
            As the counit of an adjunction, it satisfies the following
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
            can be written as a composition
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to P Y\to Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to P Y
            is deterministic (it is the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernel#kernels_from_deterministic_functions"} kernel induced by a measurable function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P Y\to Y
            is the sampling map.
          %p
            A probabilistic interpretation of this universal property is that sampling
            %em generates
            all the randomness. In other words, a process is
            %em random
            if and only if, at some point, it involves
            = succeed "," do
              %em sampling
          %p This is particularly important in probabilistic programming (see below).
          %h2#in_markov_categories In Markov categories
          %p
            (For now, see
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+category#representable_markov_categories"} representable Markov category
          %h2#in_computer_science In computer science
          %h3#in_terms_of_thunkforce_categories In terms of thunk-force categories
          %p
            As the counit of a Kleisli adjunction, the sampling map can be interpreted as the
            %em forcing map
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thunk-force+category"} thunk-force category
          %p
            One can view a probability distribution as a
            = succeed "," do
              %em thunk
            = succeed ")" do
              %em force
          %p
            Part of the correspondence between the Markov category formalism and the thunk-force formalism is studied in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#det_submonad"} Moss-Perrone’22
          %h3#in_probabilistic_programming In probabilistic programming
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                probabilistic programming
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/probabilistic+programming"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %em samp
            = succeed "," do
              %em sample
            = succeed "," do
              %em random
          %p
            The sampling map, by its universal property, can be seen as “generating” the randomness of probabilistic computations. For this reason, categorical probability can be interpreted as a form of probabilistic programming
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conditional+probability"} conditioning
          %h2#see_also See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"}> Giry monad
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monad"} probability monad
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+computer+science"} monad in computer science
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observational+monad"} observational monad
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+category"}> Markov category
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thunk-force+category"} thunk-force category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernel"}> Markov kernel
              ,
